window.TC_INDEX=[{"t":"TreeCraft User Manual","d":"Learn how to use and get the most out of TreeCraft 0.1.0 Beta.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/","g":"User manual","x":"This manual explains how to start TreeCraft, grow your first tree from generator nodes, and find help when something doesn't behave the way you expect. TreeCraft with a pine tree in the viewport and its node graph on the right images/latest/tree.png Installation TreeCraft is built from its own project folder on your computer. To build it and start it, follow Installing and running install and run . Get started If you're new to TreeCraft: Read About TreeCraft about treecraft to learn how TreeCraft thinks about trees. Take the tour of the TreeCraft window interface . Grow your first tree with Building a tree with generators generators . Keep Keyboard and mouse shortcuts shortcuts close while you work. Support If something doesn't work as described: Check the Release notes release notes to see whether the feature is in this version yet. Press the ? button at the bottom left of the viewport for quick tips. Look up the page for the panel you're using in the Documentation map doc map . More TreeCraft documentation Documentation map doc map : every page of this manual in one list. Previous versions TreeCraft 0.1.0 Beta is the first version, so there is no older documentation. "},{"t":"Documentation map","d":"Every page of the TreeCraft manual, with a line about what each covers.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/doc-map/","g":"User manual","x":"Use this map to find the page you need. Pages are listed in the order they appear in the sidebar. User manual TreeCraft User Manual home : Learn how to use and get the most out of TreeCraft 0.1.0 Beta. Documentation map doc map : Every page of the TreeCraft manual, with a line about what each covers. About TreeCraft about treecraft : What TreeCraft is, who it is for and how it thinks about trees. Installing and running install and run : Build TreeCraft on your computer and start it. Basics The TreeCraft window interface : A tour of the menus, panels, toolbar and tabs. Keyboard and mouse shortcuts shortcuts : Every key and mouse action TreeCraft understands. Preferences preferences : Change colours, viewport options, lighting and wind defaults. Modeling Building a tree with generators generators : Grow a tree from Tree, Trunk, Branch and Leaf nodes in the Generation tab. Generation modes generation modes : How Classic, Absolute, Interval and Phyllotaxy decide where children grow. Working with properties properties : Property tabs, number fields, variance and curves. Materials and meshes Materials materials : Give bark and leaves their colour, transparency, shine and variation. Leaf meshes and the cutout editor meshes : Shape leaf cards, set their pivot and anchors, and manage levels of detail. Viewing The viewport viewport : Look at your tree in perspective or orthographic views and change how it is drawn. Wind, season, forces and collision scene : Scene tools that bend, sway, tint and tidy your tree. Saving and exporting Saving and exporting files and export : Save your work as a TreeCraft file and export meshes for your game. Release notes Release notes release notes : What is in this version of TreeCraft and what is still to come. "},{"t":"About TreeCraft","d":"What TreeCraft is, who it is for and how it thinks about trees.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/about-treecraft/","g":"User manual","x":"TreeCraft is a procedural tree modeler for games. Instead of sculpting a tree by hand, you describe how it grows. A trunk rises from the ground, branches sprout along the trunk, and leaves cover the branches. TreeCraft builds the 3D model from those rules. How TreeCraft thinks about trees Every tree is a chain of generators . Each generator makes one kind of part and places it on its parent: Generator What it makes Tree The root of every tree. It holds global settings such as the shape radius. Trunk One or more trunks that grow up from the ground. Branch Branches that grow out of a trunk or out of other branches. Leaf Leaf cards placed on branches, or on other leaves. Change one number on a generator and every part it made updates at once. To give a tree more branches, raise the branch generator's frequency. To make them droop, add gravity. Custom generation, no presets TreeCraft starts with an empty scene that holds only the Tree node. You build every tree yourself from generators. There are no ready made tree presets to start from. What you can do with TreeCraft Build trees in a node graph in the Generation tab. Edit each part in the Properties panel on the left. Make bark and leaf materials in the Materials tab. Draw leaf shapes, pivots and anchors in the Meshes tab and the cutout editor. Look at the tree in perspective or orthographic views, with many drawing modes. Add wind, season colours, forces and leaf collision. Save your work and export the tree as an OBJ mesh for your game engine. Where to go next The TreeCraft window interface Building a tree with generators generators "},{"t":"Installing and running","d":"Build TreeCraft on your computer and start it.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/install-and-run/","g":"User manual","x":"TreeCraft is a desktop program written in Rust. You build it once from its project folder, then start it like any other program. Build TreeCraft 1. Install the Rust toolchain from rustup.rs if it isn't on your computer yet. 2. Open a terminal in TreeCraft's app folder. 3. Run cargo build . The first build downloads TreeCraft's libraries and takes a few minutes. Later builds are quicker. Start TreeCraft Run the program the build made: target/debug/treecraft on Windows, treecraft.exe . TreeCraft opens with an empty scene that holds only the Tree node. TreeCraft right after it starts, with only the Tree node in the Generation tab images/latest/start screen.png Options for testing TreeCraft also understands two options that help when checking a build: Option What it does screenshot <file.png Draws about 120 frames, saves a picture of the window and exits. self test <file.json Drives the program with scripted mouse input orbit the camera, select a node, drag a property, add a node, switch tabs and writes the results to the file. Where TreeCraft keeps your settings Your preferences are saved in %APPDATA%/TreeCraft/prefs.txt on Windows. To go back to the default settings, delete this file while TreeCraft is closed. "},{"t":"The TreeCraft window","d":"A tour of the menus, panels, toolbar and tabs.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/interface/","g":"Basics","x":"The TreeCraft window has four main areas: the menu bar along the top, the Properties panel on the left, the viewport in the middle and the dock on the right. The TreeCraft window: Properties on the left, the viewport and its toolbar in the middle, the Generation tab on the right images/latest/tree.png Menu bar Menu What's in it File New, Open, Recent files, Close, Save, Save incremental, Save as, export commands and Exit. Edit Undo and Redo each names the step it will undo or redo , Preferences and window properties. Tools Ambient occlusion, collision primitives, scene scale and unit conversion, and asset clean up. Window Show or hide panels, and reset the layout. Assets Materials and meshes used by the tree. Help Tips and information about TreeCraft. Every tree you open gets a tab above the viewport. Use + next to the tabs to start another tree. Properties panel The Properties panel shows the settings of whatever is selected: a generator, a material or a mesh. When nothing is selected it shows shortcuts: Wind , Lighting , Global properties and Window properties . Asset Browser : your saved assets. Drag one into the Generation tab to add it to the tree. PivotPainter2 : settings for exporting pivot data for wind animation in game engines. New tree , Open... , Save and Save As... . The four buttons at the top of the panel switch what you edit: Generator : the rules of the selected generator available in this version . Node , Freehand and Export : planned editing modes see the Release notes release notes . Properties are grouped in collapsible sections. To learn how to change them, see Working with properties properties . Viewport and toolbar The viewport shows your tree. The toolbar above it has four groups: Group Buttons View Render how the tree is drawn , Show which parts are visible , Zoom framing the camera . Scene Forces , Scene objects , Collision Objects , Wind , Light . Edit Generators / Nodes , Season , Add , Visibility , Gizmo . Post AO ambient occlusion , Collision leaf collision , Export . The < button at the end of the toolbar folds it away to save space. The bar under the toolbar switches between the perspective view and the orthographic views. The bottom of the viewport counts triangles, vertices, branches and leaves. Read more in The viewport viewport and Wind, season, forces and collision scene . Dock The dock on the right has three tabs switch between them with the tabs at the bottom of the dock : Generation : the node graph where you build the tree. See Building a tree with generators generators . Materials : bark and leaf materials. See Materials materials . Meshes : leaf shapes and their levels of detail. See Leaf meshes and the cutout editor meshes . To resize the panels, drag the gap between a panel and the viewport. "},{"t":"Keyboard and mouse shortcuts","d":"Every key and mouse action TreeCraft understands.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/shortcuts/","g":"Basics","x":" Files and editing Key Action Ctrl+N New tree Ctrl+O Open Ctrl+S Save Ctrl+Shift+S Save as Ctrl+W Close the tree Ctrl+E Export mesh Ctrl+Z Undo Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Viewport Key or mouse Action Left drag Orbit Middle drag or Shift+drag Pan Right drag or wheel Zoom F Frame the tree Z Zoom menu Number pad 0 Perspective view Number pad 1 / 3 / 7 Front / Right / Top view Number pad 5 Switch perspective/orthographic Show and hide Key Action 1 Leaves 2 Fronds 3 Branches 4 Zones 5 Targets 9 Proxies 6 Forces 7 Collision objects 8 Wind on or off H Hide or show the selection I Isolate the selection Tools Key Action Tab Switch between Generators and Nodes Alt+S Season O Ambient occlusion Generation tab Mouse Action Click a node Select it and show its properties Right click Open the Add menu, or the menu for a node Drag from a socket onto a node Link to a new parent Cutout editor Mouse Action Click outside the shape Add an outline point Click inside the shape Add an inner point Right click an anchor Pick the anchor's number Middle drag Pan the canvas Middle+left drag Zoom the canvas "},{"t":"Preferences","d":"Change colours, viewport options, lighting and wind defaults.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/preferences/","g":"Basics","x":"Open Edit Preferences to change how TreeCraft looks and behaves. Preferences are saved automatically in %APPDATA%/TreeCraft/prefs.txt . Themes 3D Viewport colours: the background top and bottom , the grid and every fifth grid line, the X and Z axes, labels, bones, normals, scribed edges, forces, collision objects, the picked object and the corner axes. Background brightness : from 0 to 3. A colour picker with a hue bar, a saturation/brightness square, and R, G, B and A values. Presets : save your colours under a name, then load or delete them later. Viewport Lighting, wireframe, texture mapping, hiding back faces, alpha testing, the grid and view labels. Also: Zoom distance scale : how far the camera stands back when it frames the tree. Manipulator size : how close a click must be to pick a scene object. Snapping : translation, rotation and scale snapping, each with its own step. Field of view . Lighting The sun's direction and height, ambient occlusion and whether lighting is on. Wind Whether wind is on, its strength and its direction. The tree sways in that direction. Global The Tree generator's shape radius, the preview level of detail and tree statistics. "},{"t":"Building a tree with generators","d":"Grow a tree from Tree, Trunk, Branch and Leaf nodes in the Generation tab.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/generators/","g":"Modeling","x":"You build every tree in the Generation tab of the dock. Each circle in the graph is a generator. Lines join each generator to its parent. Read the graph from the bottom up: the Tree node sits at the bottom, with the trunk, the branches and finally the leaves above it. A pine made of a trunk and several branch generators. The numbers on the links show how many parts each one made. images/latest/tree.png Grow your first tree 1. Select the Tree node at the bottom of the Generation tab. 2. Choose + Add or right click the graph and pick a trunk. A trunk grows from the ground. 3. Select the trunk node and add a branch generator. Branches appear along the trunk. 4. Select the branch node and add a leaf generator. Leaves cover the branches. 5. Click any node to see and change its settings in the Properties panel. The small number on each link shows how many parts the generator above it made: for example, 1 trunk and 220 branches. To keep a big graph tidy, generators can sit inside a named frame, such as \"Right branch\" in the picture above. Working in the graph To do this Do this Select a generator Click its node. Add a generator Select its future parent, then choose + Add or right click the graph. Move a generator to a new parent Drag from a node's socket onto the new parent or Shift+drag the node onto it . Open more options for a node Right click the node. Try a new random tree Choose Randomize in the dock's toolbar. The Add menu lists TreeCraft's generator types by name trunks, branches, leaf meshes and more . Each entry makes the closest generator TreeCraft has, named after the entry. Entries that TreeCraft can't build yet say so. From file... loads generator settings from a template file. New generators start plain A generator you add starts with neutral settings, so you can see exactly what each setting does as you change it: no noise, kinks or gravity no random variation in length, angle or radius no flare, droop, fold, curl or twist no material, so it draws in plain grey The generator types Tree The root of the graph. Its Shape radius 10 by default sets the overall size used by global settings. Trunk Grows straight up from the ground. A typical trunk is 25 units long with a radius of 1. Its start angle of 0.5 means vertical. Trunks taper toward the tip and can flare at the base. Branch Grows from a trunk or another branch. First and Last set where along the parent the branches may appear, from 0 the base to 1 the tip . Length and radius can be fixed values or a share of the parent's for example 40% of the parent's length . A selected Branch generator: its property tabs and Generation settings on the left, and the branches it made highlighted in the viewport images/latest/branch.png Leaf Places leaf cards on branches. Each leaf uses a mesh from the Meshes tab and a material from the Materials tab. Leaves can droop, fold along the midrib, curl along their length and turn toward the sky. A Leaf generator placed under another Leaf generator grows one leaf on each green anchor of the parent leaf's mesh. This is how you make clusters and compound leaves. See Leaf meshes and the cutout editor meshes anchors . Related pages Generation modes generation modes Working with properties properties "},{"t":"Generation modes","d":"How Classic, Absolute, Interval and Phyllotaxy decide where children grow.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/generation-modes/","g":"Modeling","x":"A generator's Mode in the Gen tab of its properties decides how many children it makes and where they sit on the parent. This page covers the four main modes. Classic Makes a number of children on each parent and scatters them between First and Last . Style: Absolute makes the whole number part of Frequency on each parent. Style: Relative makes more children on longer parents parent length × Frequency × 0.025 . Fractions are dropped, not rounded. Balanced spaces the children evenly around the parent. Without it they point in random directions. Offset adds randomness to the spacing. Sweep limits how far around the parent they spread 1 = all the way round . Roll turns them all together. Absolute Makes exactly Number children on each parent, spaced evenly around it. Jumble adds randomness. Sweep and Roll work as in Classic. A trunk usually uses Absolute with Number 1. Interval Walks down the parent from Last to First in regular steps and puts a cluster of children at each step: Frequency sets how often a step happens a higher value means shorter steps . Count is the number of children at each step, spread evenly around the parent. Spiral turns each step a little further round than the last. Spread jitters the children along the parent. Limit caps the number of steps. When you switch a generator to Interval, TreeCraft converts its frequency so the tree keeps a similar density. Phyllotaxy Places children at regular nodes along the parent, the way real plants arrange leaves. Settings: internode length distance between nodes , children per node, spread, spiral and limit. Choosing a mode You want Use A natural scatter of branches Classic One trunk, or an exact count Absolute Whorls of branches at regular heights, like a conifer Interval Leaves in pairs or spirals along a twig Phyllotaxy Note: The Generation seed changes the random choices without changing the rules. Use Randomize to try a new seed. "},{"t":"Working with properties","d":"Property tabs, number fields, variance and curves.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/properties/","g":"Modeling","x":"When you click a generator in the Generation tab, its settings appear in the Properties panel on the left. This page explains how the panel is organised and how to change values. Property tabs Generators have many settings, so they are sorted into tabs. Branch and trunk generators Tab What it controls Gen Generation: mode, frequency, where children appear. Spine The centre line: length, start angle, gravity, noise, kinks and forces. Shape Shape control. Skin Radius, taper, flare and the type of surface. UV How the bark texture wraps around the branch. Displacement Surface displacement settings. Material Material, season and vertex colour settings. Vertex Color Colours stored on the mesh's vertices for use in game shaders. Animation Wind and growth. Segments How many sides and steps the mesh has. LOD Level of detail settings. Hand Drawn Settings for branches drawn by hand. The property tabs of a Branch generator above its Generation settings images/latest/branch.png Leaf generators Tab What it controls Gen Generation and anchors. Skin Leaf size, lighting and deformation. Orientation Facing the sky, droop and final adjustments. Lighting Lighting and material settings. Material Season, leaf type and vertex colour. LOD Level of detail settings. Animation Wind and growth. Collision Leaf collision. The All tab lists everything, including random seeds. Names shown faintly are kept with the tree and saved in your file, but don't change the model in this version yet. Changing a value Drag across a number field to change it smoothly, like turning a dial. Click a number to select it, then type a new value. Press Ctrl+A or double click to select it again. Use the arrows to step the value up or down. Whole numbers step by 1. Other values step by a round amount such as 0.01 or 0.5 that suits the setting. Choices such as Mode or Style open a list. Every finished change can be undone with Ctrl+Z and redone with Ctrl+Y . Undo has no limit. Variance Rows with a +/ button can vary from part to part, so no two branches look exactly the same. Open Variance Settings with that button: Setting Meaning Variance How far each part may stray from the value. Distribution Uniform any amount is equally likely or Gaussian most parts stay close to the value . Cohesion From Cohesive neighbouring parts vary together to Random each part varies on its own . Scale, Offset The size and starting point of the cohesive pattern. Curves Some properties can change along the tree with a curve. Open the curve editor from the property's row. A Parent curve changes the value depending on where the part sits on its parent. A Distribution blue or Profile yellow curve changes the value along the part's own length. In the curve editor you can pick a preset shape Max, Min, Linear growth, Exp decay, Bell, S Curve, Droop and more , draw freely, or move points by hand. Right click for more commands: insert a smooth, step or corner point, delete points, flip or scale the curve, and copy or paste curves. A curve always keeps at least two points. UV mapping The UV tab controls how bark wraps the branch: Style : Unrestricted, U locked to 0–1, or U and V locked to 0–1. U Absolute and Relative set how many times the texture repeats around the branch. Offset and Twist turn it. V Style sets how the texture runs along the branch: Absolute, Relative keeping the aspect ratio, or Relative to the branch's length. Random flip flips the twist on about half of the branches. "},{"t":"Materials","d":"Give bark and leaves their colour, transparency, shine and variation.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/materials/","g":"Materials and meshes","x":"Materials give your tree its look: the bark's colour and bumps, and the leaves' colour, transparency and glow when light shines through them. You make and edit them in the Materials tab of the dock. The Materials tab with a pine bark material: the preview at the top, Settings below, then the texture slots. The material's settings also show in the Properties panel on the left. images/latest/materials.png The top row Control What it does Drag handle Drag onto the viewport to give a generator this material. Material picker Chooses the material to edit. It shows \"No materials\" when there are none. +/ Opens the Materials window: add, duplicate, rename, reorder or remove materials, then OK or Cancel . + Browse for texture files. Each file becomes a new material. Copy/paste menu Copies one material's settings onto another. Preview The preview shows the current material on a Sphere or a Card . Drag to turn it, use the mouse wheel to zoom, and double click to reset the view. Settings Main : Two sided draws both faces of a leaf , Season colour, and Make New Set... to start a material set from this material. Variations : three colours that tint leaves at random, and an Amount slider that controls how strong the tint is. Atlas : Scale , and whether the material is forced into or out of a texture atlas. Other : Max resolution Default, Original, 4096 down to 256 and User data . Texture slots Each material has ten slots. Each slot holds a value and an optional texture: Slot What it controls Color The base colour. Opacity Transparency. Areas below the alpha cutoff are cut away. Normal Fine surface bumps. Roughness How rough or smooth the surface is. Lower values look shinier. Specular How strong the highlights are. Metallic How metal like the surface is. Subsurface The colour of light passing through a leaf. Subsurface % How much light passes through. AO Built in shadowing in cracks. Custom A spare slot for your own data. The same settings appear in the Properties panel when the material is selected. They're grouped there as General, Color tint, texture file, brightness, contrast, saturation, hue shift , Opacity, Normal, Roughness / Specular, Subsurface, AO and Variation. Meshes sub tab A leaf material can carry a cutout mesh : a leaf shape that follows the opaque pixels of its texture so fewer see through pixels are drawn. In the Meshes sub tab you pick that mesh, choose Edit... to open the cutout editor, or use the arrow to jump to the mesh in the Meshes tab. Add attaches extra meshes. See Leaf meshes and the cutout editor meshes . "},{"t":"Leaf meshes and the cutout editor","d":"Shape leaf cards, set their pivot and anchors, and manage levels of detail.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/meshes/","g":"Materials and meshes","x":"Leaves in TreeCraft are flat cards or small meshes. In the Meshes tab you choose each leaf's shape, how many points it has at each level of detail, and where it attaches to the branch. The Meshes tab showing a pine leaf cutout with three levels of detail images/latest/meshes.png The top row Drag handle : drop on the viewport to give the selected Leaf generator or the first one this mesh. Mesh picker : the mesh you're editing. +/ : opens the Meshes window to add, duplicate, rename, reorder or remove meshes. Changes apply when you press OK . + : loads an OBJ file as a new mesh. Copy, Paste and Duplicate. The Meshes window for managing leaf meshes images/latest/mesh dialog.png New meshes get unique names automatically: \"Mesh\", \"Mesh 2\", \"Mesh 3\" and so on. Levels of detail Each mesh has three levels, High , Med and Low , so distant trees can use simpler leaves. Each level is either a cutout drawn in TreeCraft or an OBJ file: ... Browse loads a file into the level. When you load a High file, TreeCraft can look for matching \"lod1\", \"lod2\" files next to it. Reload reads the file again. Export saves the level as an OBJ file. An empty level uses the level above it. More lets another mesh supply the levels below Low. The V: and F: numbers count the vertices and faces. A: counts the anchors. Transform and other settings Setting Meaning Orient Which way is up in the file: Z Up or Y Up, right or left handed, or any axis order. Flip Mirror the mesh on X, Y, Z or a combination. Flip normals / Fix winding Repair meshes that look inside out. Wind direction Growth : the axis that points along the leaf +Y by default . Ripple : the direction used for wind ripples. Proxy collision Scale and weight kept for game use. Collection Builds the mesh from the branches marked \"Contribute to collections\". The cutout editor Choose Edit Mesh... to open Edit Cutout , where you draw the leaf's outline over its texture. The Edit Cutout window: tools along the top, the meshes list, Auto cutout and Export on the left, the leaf texture with its pivot line in the middle, and High, Med and Low previews on the right images/latest/cutout a.png Meshes list The list on the left shows every mesh with its point count. Click one to edit it, or choose New mesh to start another. Auto cutout Auto cutout traces the outline of the opaque parts of the texture for you. Re generate traces it again after you change the settings: Setting Meaning Alpha How opaque a pixel must be to count as part of the leaf. Join gap Joins separate parts that are closer than this. Min size Ignores specks smaller than this. Points How many outline points to use. Margin Extra space kept around the leaf. Concavity How closely the outline follows inward curves. Skip straight parts Leaves out points on straight stretches. Variant Tries a different version of the outline. Export Saves the mesh as FBX , OBJ or XML , with its origin at the pivot and growing along +Y. Choose Export mesh... to pick where to save it. Points Click outside the shape to add an outline point. It joins the nearest point without crossing the outline. Click inside the shape to add an inner point that gives the leaf more detail. Drag points to move them. Use x to remove points and R to reset them TreeCraft asks before resetting . Pivot The Pivot white, with an orange angle line is where the leaf attaches to the branch and the direction it grows. Drag it, or set its X, Y and angle in the Pivot group. Anchors Green anchors mark spots where more leaves can grow. A Leaf generator placed under another Leaf generator grows one leaf on each green anchor, pointing along the anchor's line. Right click an anchor to give it a number. The child generator's Anchors setting can then use all anchors or only the ones with a given number. If no anchor matches, the status bar shows: \"Unable to find anchor for node\". Check the anchors on every level , First/Last and the anchor number. Transform and Color M , R and S move, rotate and scale the whole card,"},{"t":"The viewport","d":"Look at your tree in perspective or orthographic views and change how it is drawn.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/viewport/","g":"Viewing","x":"The viewport in the middle of the window shows your tree as you build it. It opens in a Perspective view. You can switch to flat orthographic views when you need exact side or top views. Moving the camera Action Mouse or key Orbit around the tree Left drag Pan Middle drag or Shift+drag Zoom Right drag or mouse wheel Frame the whole tree F, or Zoom Selected Views Use the menu under the toolbar, or the number pad: View Number pad Perspective 0 Front 1 Right 3 Top 7 Switch perspective/orthographic 5 Front, Back, Left, Right, Top and Bottom are orthographic: there is no perspective, so sizes and angles can be measured by eye. The axis widget in the corner shows which way X, Y and Z point. Render modes Render changes how the tree is drawn. Standard shows the finished look. Other modes help you check your work: Scribed : draws the triangle edges over a plain surface. AO : shows only the ambient occlusion. Albedo Check and Subsurface Check : colour values that are too dark show in blue, and values that are too bright show in red. Lighting : shows one part of the lighting untextured, diffuse, specular, ambient, subsurface or shadow . Material : shows one material slot colour, opacity, gloss, normal and so on . Vertex Colors : shows the colour, or the red, green or blue channel alone. Other : classic shading models Phong, Blinn Phong, Oren Nayar, Lambert, Flat and diagnostic views depth, overdraw, texture coordinates and seams . Show Show turns parts on and off: leaves key 1 , branches 3 , the grid, bones the centre line of every branch , normals, and two sided rendering. Zoom and visibility Zoom : frame everything or only the selection, and copy, paste or reset the camera. Visibility : hide the selected parts H , isolate them I , or show everything again. Light Light turns lighting on or off and opens the light's properties, such as the sun's direction and height. Statistics The bottom of the viewport counts Triangles , Vertices , Branches and Leaves . Keep an eye on them to stay within your game's budget. Tip: The ? button at the bottom left of the viewport shows quick tips. "},{"t":"Wind, season, forces and collision","d":"Scene tools that bend, sway, tint and tidy your tree.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/scene/","g":"Viewing","x":"The Scene , Edit and Post groups of the viewport toolbar add life and polish to your tree. Forces Add a Direction or Magnet force to bend every branch while it grows, the same way gravity does. A strength of 1 bends about as much as a gravity of 1. A magnet's pull gets weaker with distance. Press 6 to show or hide forces in the viewport. Scene objects and the gizmo Click a scene object in the viewport to pick it, then use Gizmo : Translate moves it. Rotate turns its direction. Scale changes its strength or size. Snapping for each tool can be set in Preferences preferences . Collision objects Collision Objects adds Sphere and Capsule shapes. They are for collision in your game, so they are drawn but don't change the tree. Press 7 to show or hide them. Tools Generate collision primitives places three capsules along the trunk for you. Wind Wind sways the tree by height and flutters its leaves. The presets are Calm , Breeze , Strong and Gale . Press 8 to switch wind on or off. Set the default strength and direction in Preferences preferences . Season Season Alt+S tints leaf materials across the year, on a scale from 0 to 1: Season value Leaf colour 0 – 0.25 Fresh spring green 0.25 – 0.5 The material's own colour 0.5 – 0.85 Turning toward orange 0.85 – 1 Brown Ambient occlusion AO key O darkens leaves and branches deep inside the crown, where little light reaches. Leaf collision Collision removes leaves that sit too close to leaves already placed, so the crown doesn't look crowded. Choose High , Medium or Low how strictly overlapping leaves are removed or None . "},{"t":"Saving and exporting","d":"Save your work as a TreeCraft file and export meshes for your game.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/files-and-export/","g":"Saving and exporting","x":" Saving your tree TreeCraft saves trees in its own text format with the .tctree extension. The file keeps everything: every generator setting, including the faint ones not used yet materials, with their settings and texture file names leaf meshes, with their levels, outlines, anchors and colours scene objects Command Shortcut What it does New Ctrl+N Starts an empty tree. Open Ctrl+O Opens a .tctree file. Save Ctrl+S Saves to the current file. Save as Ctrl+Shift+S Saves to a new file. Save incremental Saves a numbered copy tree 001, tree 002 and so on next to the current file. Close Ctrl+W Closes the tree. Recent files remembers the last 16 trees you opened. If the tree has unsaved changes when you close it, start a new one or open another, TreeCraft asks whether to save: Yes saves first. No discards the changes. Cancel keeps the tree open. Exporting for your game Export in the Post group, or File Export mesh , Ctrl+E writes the tree as a Wavefront OBJ file with positions, texture coordinates and normals. Each material gets its own group in the file, so your game engine can give each part its own material. You can also export a single leaf level from the Meshes tab, and render images of the tree. Export image sequence saves 36 pictures in one full turn around the tree. Units Tools Scene unit conversion rescales the tree between units: feet to metres × 0.3048 , inches to centimetres × 2.54 and metres to centimetres × 100 . "},{"t":"Release notes","d":"What is in this version of TreeCraft and what is still to come.","u":"/TreeCraft/en-us/treecraft/1/release-notes/","g":"Release notes","x":" TreeCraft 0.1.0 Beta The first version of TreeCraft. New Node graph in the Generation tab, with Tree, Trunk, Branch and Leaf generators. Generation modes : Classic, Absolute, Interval and Phyllotaxy. Properties panel with tabs for each generator type, number fields you can drag, variance settings and a curve editor with presets. Materials tab with ten texture slots, colour variations, material sets, and a sphere or card preview. Meshes tab with High, Med and Low levels, OBJ import and export, orientation and flip settings, and the Meshes window. Cutout editor with outline and inner points, a pivot, numbered anchors for leaves on leaves, a transform gizmo, vertex colour painting, tessellation and improved folding. Viewport in perspective with orthographic views, more than 30 render modes, show and hide toggles, visibility tools and camera framing. Scene tools : direction and magnet forces, sphere and capsule collision objects, wind presets, season tinting, ambient occlusion and leaf collision. Files : the .tctree format, recent files, incremental saves, unsaved changes prompts and OBJ export. Preferences for viewport colours with presets, viewport options and snapping, lighting, wind and global settings. Unlimited undo and redo . Asset Browser for reusing saved assets, and PivotPainter2 settings. Auto cutout in the cutout editor, and mesh export as FBX, OBJ or XML. Named frames for grouping generators in the node graph. Roughness slot for materials. Known limitations Only Generator editing works. Node and Freehand editing are planned. There is no Displacements tab yet, and TreeCraft has no frond generator. The Classic, Absolute, Interval and Phyllotaxy modes are available. Other generation modes Proportional, Bifurcation, Flood and more are not. Some properties are stored and saved but don't change the tree yet. They show with faint names. Some menu items cameras from FBX, the growth wizard, the timeline tell you they aren't available in this version. "}];